<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Si Inthrathit</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Pho Khun Si Inthrathit, Bang Klang Hao</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Si Inthrathit, originally called Bang Klang Hao, was the founder of the Sukhothai Kingdom in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. With his ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pha-mueang">Pho Khun Pha Mueang</a> he drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> governor out of Sukhothai in the mid-13th century and became its king, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phra-ruang-dynasty">Phra Ruang dynasty</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-khamhaeng">Ram Khamhaeng</a> extended Sukhothai's influence across much of the region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/si-inthrathit.jpg" alt="Si Inthrathit" width="320" height="507" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: th:ผู้ใช้:Omogoki · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E0%B8%9E%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%82%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%A8%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%B4%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%97%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%97%E0%B8%B4%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%A2%E0%B9%8C_%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%84%E0%B8%B8%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B5_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Si Inthrathit was born to the royal family of Sukhothai, though his exact lineage is unclear. Legend makes him a descendant of King Patumsuriyawong, whom some historians identify with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suryavarman-ii">Suryavarman II</a>. Before he became king he was chief of Bang Yang, a territory in what is now north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> that then lay in the westernmost part of the Khmer Empire. He was first known as Pho Khun Bang Klang Hao, interpreted as the lord who rules the sky. Inscriptions are often missing their top or bottom portions, where dates would stand, and this uncertainty over the name led to his being called simply Hao in his early life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Bang Klang Hao and Khun Pha Mueang of Mueang Rat decided to rebel and declare independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>. Khmer control and its heavy taxes were the main motives, and the Khmer preoccupation with great architectural works weakened their defences and indirectly helped the rising. Bang Klang Hao captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/si-satchanalai">Si Satchanalai</a> and gave it to Pha Mueang, who in return gave him Sukhothai.</p><p>He was then declared king at Sukhothai and took a regnal name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> origin, Si Inthrathit, translated from Adityan Indra. His skill and bravery impressed the people, who gave him the title Phra Ruang, meaning glorious prince. The title passed to all later rulers of Sukhothai, which gave rise to the first Thai royal dynasty, the Phra Ruang.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Si Inthrathit and his queen, Sueang, had three sons. The eldest died young, and the second was named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ban-mueang">Ban Mueang</a>. The third defeated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> prince in combat on elephants and was named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-khamhaeng">Ram Khamhaeng</a>, meaning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> the Bold, in tribute to the feat. Si Inthrathit died around 1270 and was succeeded by Ban Mueang. His dynasty is regarded as the first historical Siamese dynasty, both because it arose in the region foreigners later called Siam and because it was the royal house that freed the Thai principalities from the Khmer Empire.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Si Inthrathit” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to later inscriptions, two Tai chiefs, Pho Khun <b>Bang Klang Hao</b> of Mueang Bang Yang and Pho Khun Pha Mueang of Mueang Rat, joined forces to expel the Khmer governor of Sukhothai.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sukhothai-kingdom">Sukhothai Kingdom · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nakhon Thai, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. Died in Sukhothai, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=17.1,100.84&amp;year=1264" aria-label="Nakhon Thai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7292 1529 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Si Inthrathit"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7742 1799h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7742 1799h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=17.1,100.84&amp;year=1264">What was here in 1264? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
